                <h3 id="crs">CRS Station Codes</h3>
                <p><abbr title="Computer Reservation System">CRS</abbr> station codes are available from the following
                    endpoint:</p>
                <p>
                    <code>
        <a href="/crs">/crs/{query}</a>
    </code>
</p>
<p>
    If <code>query</code> is omitted then all CRS codes are returned along with their respective station names.
    <br/>
    If <code>query</code> is provided then only station names matching it will be returned along with their CRS codes.
</p>
<details>
    <summary>Example response for<code>/crs/peter</code></summary>
    <pre>
[
  {
    "stationName": "Peterborough",
    "crsCode": "PBO"
  },
  {
    "stationName": "Petersfield",
    "crsCode": "PTR"
  }
]
</pre>
                </details>
                <p>
                    <code>
        <a href="/crs/london terminals">/crs/london terminals</a>
    </code> returns all codes in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_station_group">London station group</a>
</p>
<h3 id="stations">Station Locations</h3>
<p><abbr title="Station Locations">Station</abbr> locations are available from the following endpoint:</p>
<p>
    <code>
        <a href="/stations">/stations/{query}</a>
    </code>
</p>
<p>
    If <code>query</code> is omitted then all stations are returned along with their respective station names and geo-coordinates.
    <br/>
    If <code>query</code> is provided then only station names matching it will be returned along with their CRS codes and geo-coordinates.
</p>
<details>
    <summary>Example response for<code>/stations/peter</code></summary>
    <pre>
[
  {
    "stationName": "Peterborough",
    "crsCode": "PBO",
    "latitude":52.57497366063303,
    "longitude":-0.24983696937880237
  },
  {
    "stationName": "Petersfield",
    "crsCode": "PTR",
    "latitude":51.006724600922816,
    "longitude":-0.9411367352535371
  }
]
</pre>
</details>
<p>
    <code>
        <a href="/stations/london terminals">/stations/london terminals</a>
    </code> returns all station locations in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_station_group">London station group</a>
</p>
<h3 id="service">Service</h3>
<p>
    <code>
        <a href="/service/_vEUZIJWLTtViDnv5GVpuw">/service/{Service ID}</a>
    </code>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The service ID can be found for each service inside the departures and arrivals response.
                    Huxley also returns the ID in URL percent encoded, GUID and <a
                        href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Base64#URL_applications">URL safe Base64</a> representations
                    (for non-staff boards).
                    Likewise, the service endpoint will accept <a
                        href="https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc4648#section-5">URL safe Base64</a> service IDs, from
                    various different encoders.
                </p>
                <p>
                    This endpoint also accepts the <a href="/service/46715300-0000-0000-4248-414d4e57535f">GUID
                        representation of the ID</a>
                </p>
                <p>
                    If the ID is a RID (a 15 digit long integer) then the staff API will be used. In this case a staff
                    access token must be used (unless configured server side).
                </p>

<h3 id="ojp">Journey Planner</h3>
<p>
    <code>
        <a href="/api/planner/WTN/GLC/2026-06-01T08:00:00?itemChoiceType=1&enquiryType=0">/api/planner/{CRS|Origin}/{CRS|Destination}/{String|DateTime}?{Int|ItemChoiceType}&{Int|enquiryType}&{CRS|via/avoid}&{Bool|directTrains}</a>
    </code>
</p>

<h3 id="postcode-planner">Postcode Journey Planner</h3>
<p>
    Plan journeys from or to a UK postcode. The server auto-detects which parameter is a postcode
    and which is a station CRS code. The OJP API discovers nearby stations and returns full journey
    plans including multi-leg routes and Underground connections.
</p>
<p>
    <code>
        <a href="/api/planner/postcode?origin=SW1A1AA&amp;destination=EUS&amp;plannedTime=2026-06-01T10:00:00">/api/planner/postcode?origin={postcode|CRS}&amp;destination={CRS|postcode}&amp;plannedTime={DateTime}</a>
    </code>
</p>
<h5>Parameters:</h5>
<ul>
    <li><code>origin</code> (required) — A UK postcode or 3-letter CRS station code</li>
    <li><code>destination</code> (required) — A UK postcode or 3-letter CRS station code (exactly one must be a postcode)</li>
    <li><code>plannedTime</code> (required) — Journey date/time in ISO 8601 format (UK local time)</li>
    <li><code>itemChoiceType</code> (optional, default 0) — Supported values: 0 = Arrive By, 1 = Depart By</li>
    <li><code>enquiryType</code> (optional, default 0) — 0 = Standard, 1 = Check Alternatives</li>
    <li><code>directTrains</code> (optional, default false) — Restrict to direct services only</li>
</ul>
<h5>Examples:</h5>
<ul>
    <li>
        Postcode as origin:
        <code><a href="/api/planner/postcode?origin=SW1A1AA&amp;destination=EUS&amp;plannedTime=2026-06-01T10:00:00">/api/planner/postcode?origin=SW1A1AA&amp;destination=EUS&amp;plannedTime=2026-06-01T10:00:00</a></code>
    </li>
    <li>
        Postcode as destination:
        <code><a href="/api/planner/postcode?origin=WAT&amp;destination=TW32LN&amp;plannedTime=2026-06-01T10:00:00&amp;itemChoiceType=1">/api/planner/postcode?origin=WAT&amp;destination=TW32LN&amp;plannedTime=2026-06-01T10:00:00&amp;itemChoiceType=1</a></code>
    </li>
</ul>
<h5>Response:</h5>
<p>
    Returns a JSON object with <code>postcodeStations</code> (nearby stations with CRS code, name, and distance in miles)
    and <code>outwardJourneys</code> (journey plans grouped by selected station, each containing legs with departure/arrival
    times, platforms, operators, fares, and service bulletins).
</p>
<h5>Constraints:</h5>
<ul>
    <li>Exactly one of origin/destination must be a UK postcode; the other must be a CRS code.</li>
    <li>Postcodes are accepted with or without spaces, in any case (e.g. "SW1A 1AA", "sw1a1aa").</li>
    <li>Some postcodes may return 404 if the OJP API has no station mappings for that area.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="nearby">Nearby Services</h3>
<p>
    Get real-time train services from stations near a GPS location in a single call.
    Returns stations ordered by distance with live departure information.
</p>
<p>
    <code>
        <a href="/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&lng=-0.1132&maxStations=3">/api/nearby?lat={latitude}&amp;lng={longitude}</a>
    </code>
</p>
<h5>Parameters:</h5>
<ul>
    <li><code>lat</code> (required) — Latitude, -90 to 90</li>
    <li><code>lng</code> (required) — Longitude, -180 to 180</li>
    <li><code>destination</code> (optional) — CRS code to filter services heading to that destination</li>
    <li><code>radius</code> (optional, default 3, max 50) — Search radius in miles</li>
    <li><code>maxStations</code> (optional, default 5, max 20) — Maximum number of stations to return</li>
    <li><code>numRows</code> (optional, default 4, max 150) — Number of services per station</li>
    <li><code>expand</code> (optional, default false) — Include calling points data for duration/arrival times</li>
</ul>
<h5>Examples:</h5>
<ul>
    <li>
        Nearby stations with services near London Waterloo:
        <code><a href="/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&lng=-0.1132">/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&amp;lng=-0.1132</a></code>
    </li>
    <li>
        With expanded calling points:
        <code><a href="/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&lng=-0.1132&maxStations=2&expand=true">/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&amp;lng=-0.1132&amp;maxStations=2&amp;expand=true</a></code>
    </li>
    <li>
        Filtered by destination:
        <code><a href="/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&lng=-0.1132&destination=CBG">/api/nearby?lat=51.5031&amp;lng=-0.1132&amp;destination=CBG</a></code>
    </li>
</ul>
<p>
    Each station in the response includes <code>stationName</code>, <code>crsCode</code>, <code>latitude</code>, <code>longitude</code>,
    <code>distanceMiles</code>, and a <code>services</code> array matching the existing station board response format.
</p>

<h3 id="auth">Authentication</h3>
<p>
    All endpoints support API key authentication via the <code>x-api-key</code> request header.
    The server supports three modes configured via the <code>Security__ApiKeyMode</code> app setting:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Off</strong> — No authentication required (default)</li>
    <li><strong>Grace</strong> — Unauthenticated requests are logged but allowed through</li>
    <li><strong>Enforce</strong> — Unauthenticated requests receive 401 (missing key) or 403 (invalid key)</li>
</ul>
<p>
    API keys are configured via <code>Security__ApiKeys__0</code>, <code>Security__ApiKeys__1</code>, etc. in app settings.
    Multiple keys are supported for key rotation.
</p>

<h3 id="credentials">Credentials &amp; Access</h3>
<p>
    Different API features require different credentials:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Station Boards (Departures, Arrivals, etc.)</strong> — Requires an NRE Darwin access token.
        Register for a <a href="https://realtime.nationalrail.co.uk/OpenLDBWSRegistration/Registration">public</a>
        or <a href="http://openldbsv.nationalrail.co.uk/self-signup/register">staff</a> token.
        Pass as <code>accessToken</code> query parameter, or configure <code>DarwinAccessToken</code> in app settings.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Journey Planner &amp; Postcode Planner</strong> — Requires a separate OJP username and password from NRE.
        Configure <code>ojpEndpoint</code>, <code>ojpUsername</code>, and <code>ojpPassword</code> in app settings.
        Both the station-to-station planner and postcode planner use the same credentials.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Nearby Services</strong> — Uses the server-configured <code>DarwinAccessToken</code> for upstream calls.
        No client-side token needed.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>Station Locations &amp; CRS Codes</strong> — No external credentials required (local data).
    </li>
</ul>

